      <workList xml:id="workDesc_1265f838">
         <work xml:id="work_84N20045">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_a64400bf">B2:102</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_85d6935a" xml:lang="de">Cantate</title>
            <title type="alternative" xml:id="title_1141e4b8" xml:lang="de">Singet Gott</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_fc663eb5">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="persName_23e7552f">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_2de44a0c"/>
            <history xml:id="history_fce7dc13">
               <p xml:id="idm69">Though the catalogue of the Breitkopf publishers (1770) assigns the cantata to a 'Scheibe, <rend xml:id="idm70" fontstyle="italic">Joh. Bat.</rend> in Coppenhagen' it must surely be Johann Adolph. The catalogue lists it as a single, sacred cantata. Unfortunately the text incipit is very short, but the first line might 'Singet Gott, lobsinget seinem Namen!' – thus belong to a text which also was used by <persName xml:id="idm71" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Georg Philipp Telemann</persName> and <persName xml:id="idm72" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Christoph Graupner</persName>. The cantata does not appear in any earlier catalogue which suggests that, though it might have been obtained when they received the estate of <persName xml:id="idm73" auth="VIAF" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" role="">Carl Gotthelf Gerlach</persName> in 1761, it had not been on sale until 1770; another possible explanation might be that Scheibe sent it to the publishers from Copenhagen sometime between 1760 and 1770. The work has been lost.</p>
               <p xml:id="idm74">Information on the present, short cantata was discovered on late 2017, hence after the publication of the printed catalogue.</p>
            </history>
            <langUsage xml:id="langUsage_1343f127">
               <language xml:id="de">German</language>
               <language xml:id="en">English</language>
            </langUsage>
